{"authored" [{"question" "q-8076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write the complex number $z=5-\\text{i}$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ giving $\\theta $ in radians to $3$ decimal places." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953226/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b62273033a0283d873734318e1bde4be523f429b1e8cc0f63988f29e53fe9b" "basis" 609 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953226/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-8076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To write our complex number in the form $z=r{\\text{e}}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ where $r=|z|$ and $\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z),$ we first calculate $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=|z| & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we can find the angle $\\theta $ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z) & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{5})| \\\\  & =2\\pi -|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{5})| \\\\  & ≈2\\pi -0.197\\,39… \\\\  & ≈6.086.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $r=\\sqrt{26}$ and $\\theta =6.086$ into $z=r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{26}\\,{\\text{e}}^{6.086\\,\\text{i}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953226/state.json" "file_sha256" "d4b62273033a0283d873734318e1bde4be523f429b1e8cc0f63988f29e53fe9b" "basis" 609 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953226/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-8076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/math